::EM U4 p32 Review 1 MultiChoice:: Read the text below and decide which answer best fits each gap. There is an example at the beginning. <b>The science of happiness</b> Some scientisis believe that asking people how happy they are is  ____ asking them about an event they've attended in the past — there’s a lot they  ____ no notice of during the experience,so how do they know? These scientists think that anyone studying happiness should pay more  ____ to people’s experiences at the time they occur, not afterwards. Other scientists say that we are actually  ____ up of our memories. They suggest that studying moment-to-moment experiences at the time  ____ too much emphasis on temporary pleasures, and that happiness goes  ____ than that. They identify three key  ____ for happiness :  pleasure, engagement (how involved we are with family, work, romance and hobbies) and meaning (how we use our personal strengths to achieve important  ____. It is interesting that,  ____ to what might be expected, pleasure seems to play the smallest part in what makes us happy. {
	~against
	~opposing
	=contrary
	~contrast
}

::EM U4 p32 Review 2 OpenCloze:: Read the text below and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap. <b>More than just a pretty face?</b> There are many stories of dolphins helping people in trouble,  ____ saving swimmers from shark attacks by gathering round them or shepherding them to safety.  ____ these stories are true, why do dolphins do it and  ____ makes them behave in this way? Scientists  ____ have studied them are not entirely sure. For dolphins to act together as a group to save humans implies that  ____ is some sort of code of ethics among dolphins, but there is little evidence  for that. The  ____ likely explanation is that they instinctively respond to the appearance of predators like sharks by herding weaker members of their own group into  ____ safe place - and there is proof that dolphins do cooperate with  ____ other to ward off danger. Maybe they just mistake swimmers for part of their group, which would mean that dolphins don’t have genuine feelings of kindness towards humans.  ____ may be that their smiling appearance simply gives people the wrong idea. {
	=It
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 3 Word formation:: Read the text below. Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits in the gap in the same line. <b>Why do they do it?</b> People are attracted to attempting extreme activities for a  ____ (VARY) of reasons. Some enjoy the challenge, others the feeling of  ____ (EXCITE) they get from testing themselves. So although their actual  ____ (MOTIVATE) may differ, people who participate in extreme sports probably share an inability to accept all the  ____ (LIMIT) that most of us think of as normal. They experience a sense of  ____ (SATISFY) with the routine of everyday life, and feel a need to push the boundaries of what is possible, to see how far they can go. Is this similar to simply being  ____ (COMPETE)? Possibly, but it may be more to do with having a dream. Of course  ____ (PROFESSION) athletes who train for extreme events like the triathlon or the marathon may hope for  ____ (FINANCE) rewards, but primarily it seems they like being different. They enjoy the feeling of being set apart from other people by their  ____ (ACHIEVE), which are things most of us would fear to do. {
	=achievements
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.0 Key word transformation:: [html]Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between two and five words, including the word given. Here is an example : <blockquote>Please don’t drive so fast — this is a dangerous road.<br> <b>MORE</b><br> Please <i>drive more slowly</i> — this is a dangerous road.</blockquote>

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.1 more irritating than:: [html]Nothing irritates me more than getting hundreds of spam emails.<br> <b>MORE</b><br> There is nothing  ____ getting hundreds of spam emails. {
	=more irritating than #comparative
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.3 so excited by/about:: [html]The tennis game was so exciting that the spectators cheered loudly at the end.<br> <b>WERE</b><br> The spectators  ____ the tennis game that they cheered loudly at the end. {
	=were so excited by
	=were so excited about #phrase :  so  .. . that  .. .
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.2 interesting for/to:: [html]I'm very interesled in learning about the culture of other countries<br> <b>IS</b><br> Learning about the culture of other countries  ____ me. {
	=is very interesting for
	=is very interesting to #change of adjective form :  interested – interesting
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.4 worried by:: [html]I find it very worrying if things go wrong on holiday.<br> <b>GET</b><br> I  ____ things going wrong on holiday. {
	=get very worried about
	=get very worried by #change of adjective to verb
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.5 boring:: [html]I get bored by people who talk too much.<br> <b>FIND</b><br> I  ____ when people talk too much. {
	=find it boring #change of adjective form :  bored – boring
}

::EM U4 p33 Review 4.6 for the first time:: [html]I had never seen a glacier before I went to Norway<br> <b>I</b><br> When I was in Norway,  ____ the first time. {
	=I saw a glacier for #I had never seen – I saw  .. . for the first time
}

::EM U42 Ultimate q1:: What's the answer to this multiple-choice question?  ____ {
	~wrong answer #feedback comment on the wrong answer
	~another wrong answer #feedback comment on this wrong answer
	=right answer #Very good!
}

::EM U42 Ultimate q2:: Deep Thought said "  ____  is the Ultimate Answer to the Ultimate Question of Life, The Universe, and Everything." {
